=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The root cause is that the FRR routing daemons bgpd and zebra have been killed on
router2 and are no longer running, even though their stale PID files remain
(/var/run/frr/bgpd.pid holds PID 57; /var/run/frr/zebra.pid holds PID 50 — both
confirmed dead with kill -0). Only mgmtd and staticd are alive on router2. On
router1, all five FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, mgmtd, bgpd, staticd) are running
normally.

Affected component: FRR bgpd (BGP daemon) on router2, along with zebra (the
kernel-route programming daemon). The /etc/frr/frr.conf on router2 is correctly
authored — it defines AS 2, eBGP neighbor 193.10.11.1 (router1, AS 1), and
advertises 200.1.1.0/24 — so this is not a configuration fault.

Mechanism: With bgpd dead on router2, the eBGP session between AS 1 (router1) and
AS 2 (router2) cannot form. Router1 therefore never receives the BGP advertisement
for 200.1.1.0/24 (pc2's subnet). Router1's FRR and kernel routing tables contain
only its two directly connected prefixes (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24) with
no path to 200.1.1.0/24. Packets from pc1 destined for pc2 (200.1.1.2) are
black-holed at router1. The reverse direction also fails because router1 has no
return path even if router2's stale kernel BGP route forwards the packet. The vtysh
"Unknown command" errors reported by the NOC occur because vtysh connects to live
daemon Unix sockets; with zebra and bgpd dead, the sockets are stale and vtysh
cannot issue any command.

Root cause: The bgpd and zebra processes on router2 were killed after FRR started,
tearing down the only eBGP session in the topology.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router2 — run `service frr restart` (or
`/usr/lib/frr/frrinit.sh restart`) — which will respawn zebra, bgpd, and watchfrr,
reload frr.conf, re-establish the eBGP session with router1, and restore the
200.1.1.0/24 route on router1 so both pc1 and pc2 regain end-to-end connectivity.
=== END ===
